Learning Journal - April 3, 2025
Today's Focus: Setting Up and Using Sass in a PHP Project

Today, I worked on integrating Sass into my PHP project while using VS Code as my code editor. The process involved several key steps and challenges, but I successfully compiled and watched my SCSS files in real time.

Key Learnings:
1. Installing Sass and Node.js
  I encountered issues running npm due to script execution policies.
  Resolved them by adjusting system settings and ensuring Sass was properly installed.
  Successfully executed sass --watch styles.scss styles.css to enable real-time compilation.

2. Implementing Sass Features in My Project
  Variables: Used to store colors and padding values.
  Mixins: Created a reusable button style with dynamic background colors.
  Extend/Inheritance: Defined a reusable box style for .card.
  Nesting: Organized styles more cleanly within .container, .card, and its child elements.

3. Applying Sass to My PHP Project
  Ensured the generated styles.css was linked properly in header.php.
  Used Sass classes in my HTML structure to reflect changes dynamically.
  Verified that my styles were successfully applied to elements like buttons and cards.

4. Challenges & Solutions:
Command Not Found Error: Initially, sass --version didn’t work. I resolved this by verifying Node.js installation and configuring system permissions.
File Not Found in Sass Compilation: Fixed by ensuring the correct path to styles.scss before running the sass --watch command.

5. Next Steps:
  Further optimize my Sass files for better maintainability.
  Explore using partials (_filename.scss) for modular styling.
  Experiment with more advanced Sass functionalities like loops and conditionals.

6. Conclusion:
  Today was a productive learning experience in setting up and using Sass effectively in my PHP project. With Sass now integrated, my styles are more structured, reusable, and scalable. 


